//LABEL(parameter-notes, EN) Parameter notes
/* NOTE(parameter-notes, EN)
	Notes (documentation) for parameters, displayed in the OpenM++ user interface.
*/

/* NOTE(max_year, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Year simulation ends
  >LOCATION	Base(MortalityDisease).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This is the year beyond which nobody can live. The hecatomb occurs
  at the beginning of the year, so if the parameter value is 2042 then everybody
  who lived through 2041 will die on 2042's new year eve.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >

  >*/
/* NOTE(max_life, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Maximum number of years of life
  >LOCATION	Base(MortalityDisease).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This is the age beyond which nobody can live. So if the parameter
  is 100 then the maximum age one can reach is 100 years, 0 day, 0 second.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >

  >*/

/* NOTE(p_population, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Population by sex, age, province (territory) and year
  >LOCATION	Base(Births).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	September 2020
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[YEARS][SEX][c_province_t][life_span_t]
  >
  >
  >SOURCE	All values (by year, sex and province/territory, age) populated as
  follows:
  >* Observed estimates for 2001-2017 from CANSIM Table 17-10-0005-01 (formerly
  Table 051-0001)
  >* Projected (M3) estimates for 2018-2043 (see ASSUMPTIONS)
  >* Imputed estimates for 2044-2100 based on 2043 estimates
  >* Zero values for age groups 100-119 for all years, sexes and
  provinces/territories
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	The population parameter has three functions/purposes:
  >(1) The first is to help assign an exact age from the age group of the case
  being initialized from the StartPop input parameter. (see parameter
  p_use_input_population_to_assign_age)
  >(2) It is used in the projection of new cases ("births" and immigrants) in
  future years.
  >(3) It can be used to "scale" the results to the population distribution (by
  age, sex and province, although this isn't necessary if the input database
  StartPop is representative of the population.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >The M3 projections (The medium-growth and 1999/2000 to 2002/2003
  interprovincial migrations trends) scenario is defined by the following
  assumptions:
  >(i) Canadian total fertility rate that reaches 1.67 births per woman in
  2021/2022 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(ii) Canadian life expectancy that reaches 87.5 years for males and 89.1 years
  for females in 2062/2063;
  >(iii) interprovincial migration based on the trends observed between 1999/2000
  and 2002/2003;
  >(iv) national immigration rate that reaches 0.75% in 2022/2023 and remains
  constant thereafter;
  >(v) annual number of non-permanent residents (Canada) that reaches 864,600 in
  2021 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(vi) National net emigration rate of 0.19%.
  >The above is from demography documentation (may be outdated, was probably
  correct circa November 2014)
  >
  >DECISIONS	In November 2014, we changed the dimensions of this parameter
  beginning in 1971 to begin in 2001.
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >*/

/* NOTE(fictive_person_characteristics, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Characteristics of a fictive person to be simulated
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter may be useful for debugging/probing
  purposes.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[db_fields_t]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION	The codesets (e.g. Sex=1 corresponds to male) are found below
  >Five year age group : see parameter p_db_age_decode (1 = 12-17, 2 = 18-19, 3 = 20-24, 4 = 25-29, ...)
  >Sex : 0 = Female, 1 = Male
  >Province : 0 = Newfoundland, 1 = PEI, 2 = Nova Scotia, 3 = New Brunswick, 4 =
  Quebec, 5 = Ontario, 6 = Manitoba, 7 = Saskatchewan, 8 = Alberta, 9 = British
  Columbia
  >Recent immigrant (last 9 years) : 0 = No, 1 = Yes
  >Education : 0 = Less than secondary, 1 = Secondary grad , 2 = Other
  post-secondary, 3 = Post-secondary grad
  >Income : 0 = Quartile 1 (poorest), 1 = Quartile 2 (lower middle), 2 = Quartile
  3 (upper middle), 3 = quartile 4 (richest)
  >Alcohol drinks past week : -2 = Never drank, -1 = Former drinker, otherwise the
  number of drinks in the past week (0, 1, 2, ...)
  >Body mass index in kg/(m^2)
  >Height in meters
  >Survey weight
  >
  >METHODS	One needs to fill the CCHS-like questionnaire above.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >*/
/* NOTE(p_immig, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Number of new immigrants by sex, age, province and year
  >LOCATION	Base(Immig).dat
  >LAST UPDATE	November 2020
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	Number of new immigrants by sex, age, province and year (aka
  p_immig) provides BMIGen with the number of immigrants by year, sex, province and
  age. This parameter is used to generate the correct number of immigrants after
  2001. In 2001 the number of immigrants is determined by the starting
  population.
  >More details in the SOURCE section below.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS	[YEARS][SEX][c_province_t][LIFE]
  >
  >SOURCE	All values (by year, sex and province/territory, age) populated as
  follows:
  >* Observed estimates for 2001-2018 from CANSIM Table 17-10-0014-01 (formerly
  Table 051-0011)
  >* Projected (M3) estimates for 2019-2042 (see ASSUMPTIONS)
  >* Imputed estimates for 2043-2100 based on 2042 estimates
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	This parameter is used to determine the distribution of new immigrants
  in future years (after p_start_year up to p_max_year_new_arrivals) by sex, age and
  year of immigration.
  >
  >The remaining characteristics of the immigrants are assigned through hot-deck
  imputation of immigrants (within the past 9 years) represented in the
  initialization database.
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS	For years 2019-2042, used official population projections from the
  cohort component model for ages 0-110. These were the medium term projections
  M3. According to the demography documentation, the medium-growth and 1999/2000
  to 2002/2003 interprovincial migrations trends scenario is defined by the
  following assumptions:
  >(i) a Canadian total fertility rate that reaches 1.67 births per woman in
  2021/2022 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(ii) a Canadian life expectancy that reaches 87.5 years for males and 89.1
  years for females in 2062/2063;
  >(iii) interprovincial migration based on the trends observed between 1999/2000
  and 2002/2003;
  >(iv) a national immigration rate that reaches 0.75% in 2022/2023 and remains
  constant thereafter;
  >(v) an annual number of non-permanent residents (Canada) that reaches 864,600
  in 2021 and remains constant thereafter;
  >(vi) National net emigration rate of 0.19%.
  >
  >DECISIONS	In November 2014, we changed the dimensions of this parameter
  beginning in 1971 to begin in 2001.
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >
  >*/
/* NOTE(p_start_pop, EN)
