  20+. Some records are removed due to missing values.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[db_records_t][db_fields_t]
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	Dummy records are filled with -1 in all fields.
  >See parameter “Characteristics of a fictive person to be simulated” for the
  list of fields/variables.
  >
  >
  >*/
/* NOTE(p_new_arrivals_control, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Open or Closed population
  >LOCATION
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	This parameter determines which type of cases will be generated by
  the simulation.
  >
  >If "StartPop database" is checked then cases will be generated in the start
  year (p_start_year) of the simulation according to the cross-sectional
  representation of the population found in the initialization database (see
  parameter p_start_pop).
  >
  >If "New Births" is checked then new cases will be generated in future years
  (up to p_max_year_new_arrivals) to reflect the growth in the Canadian population.
  Note that these cases are generated at a specific age equal to the youngest age
  in p_start_pop (uses parameter p_population)
  >
  >If "New immigrants" is checked then new cases will be generated in future
  years (up to p_max_year_new_arrivals) to reflect projected immigration patterns
  (see parameter p_immig)
  >
  >
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[c_population_source_t]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	There are 8 possible ways to populate this parameter.
  >

  >*/

/* NOTE(p_max_year_new_arrivals, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Last year for generating Future Births and Immigrants
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION	The last year for generating Future Births and Immigrants. It is used
  to decide how many cases are needed.
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >None
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS	Relations to other parameters
  >Should be greater than or equal to p_start_year.
  >There is also a parameter called "Year simulation ends" (aka max_year) which
  allows lives to be lived sufficiently long for lifetime outcomes like life
  expectancy.
  >

  >*/
/* NOTE(p_start_pop_reweight_definition_fields, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Reweight population data base: field
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[startpop_subpopulations_t][conditions_reweight_t]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	The parameter "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"
  (aka p_start_pop) comes with survey weights that may be altered through the
  following trio of parameters:
  >A: "Reweight population data base: field" (aka
  p_start_pop_reweight_definition_fields)
  >B: "Reweight population data base: acceptance range" (aka
  p_start_pop_reweight_definition_bounds)
  >C: "Reweight population data base: multiplier" (aka
  p_start_pop_reweight_multiplier)
  >
  >Parameters A and B allow one to define up to 9 subpopulations and parameter C
  allows one to specify how survey weights should be altered (multiplicatively)
  for each population. For instance, if parameters A, B and C are set up to (i)
  multiply survey weights of women (subpopulation 1) by 0.9 and (ii) multiply
  survey weights of Albertans (subpopulation 2) by 1.2, then the survey weights
  of female Albertans are multiplied by 0.9*1.2 = 1.08.
  >
  >BMIGen conducts its entire business with the altered weights.  So if Lucy the
  female Albertan has survey weight equal to 200 on the donor database (parameter
  "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"), her survey weight
  would become 216 (based on example above) and BMIGen would totally forget that
  Lucy's survey weight ever was 200 at some point.
  >
  >The parameter currently discussed/documented is A: "Reweight population data
  base: field" (aka p_start_pop_reweight_definition_fields).
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >

  >*/
/* NOTE(p_start_pop_reweight_definition_bounds, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Reweight population data base: acceptance range
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[startpop_subpopulations_t][conditions_reweight_t][c_lower_upper_bounds_t]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >

  >Parameters A and B allow one to define up to 9 subpopulations and parameter C
  allows one to specify how survey weights should be altered (multiplicatively)
  for each population. For instance, if parameters A, B and C are set up to (i)
  multiply survey weights of women (subpopulation 1) by 0.9 and (ii) multiply
  survey weights of Albertans (subpopulation 2) by 1.2, then the survey weights
  of female Albertans are multiplied by 0.9*1.2 = 1.08.
  >
  >
  >This model conducts its entire business with the altered weights.  So if Lucy the
  female Albertan has survey weight equal to 200 on the donor database (parameter
  "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"), her survey weight
  would become 216 (based on example above) and this model would totally forget that
  Lucy's survey weight ever was 200 at some point.
  >
  >The parameter currently discussed/documented is B: "Reweight population data
  base: acceptance range" (aka p_start_pop_reweight_definition_bounds).
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS

  >*/
/* NOTE(p_start_pop_reweight_multiplier, EN)
  >
  >PARAMETER	Reweight population data base: multiplier
  >LOCATION	Base(StartPop_Control).dat
  >LAST UPDATE
  >
  >DESCRIPTION
  >
  >DIMENSIONS
  >[startpop_subpopulations_t][dummy_row_or_column_t]
  >
  >SOURCE
  >
  >DEFINITION
  >
  >METHODS	The parameter "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"
  (aka p_start_pop) comes with survey weights that may be altered through the
  following trio of parameters:
  >A: "Reweight population data base: field" (aka
  p_start_pop_reweight_definition_fields)
  >B: "Reweight population data base: acceptance range" (aka
  p_start_pop_reweight_definition_bounds)
  >C: "Reweight population data base: multiplier" (aka
  p_start_pop_reweight_multiplier)
  >
  >Parameters A and B allow one to define up to 9 subpopulations and parameter C
  allows one to specify how survey weights should be altered (multiplicatively)
  for each population. For instance, if parameters A, B and C are set up to (i)
  multiply survey weights of women (subpopulation 1) by 0.9 and (ii) multiply
  survey weights of Albertans (subpopulation 2) by 1.2, then the survey weights
  of female Albertans are multiplied by 0.9*1.2 = 1.08.
  >
  >This model conducts its entire business with the altered weights.  So if Lucy the
  female Albertan has survey weight equal to 200 on the donor database (parameter
  "Distribution of population at beginning of simulation"), her survey weight
  would become 216 (based on example above) and this model would totally forget that
  Lucy's survey weight ever was 200 at some point.
  >
  >The parameter currently discussed/documented is C: "Reweight population data
  base: multiplier" (aka p_start_pop_reweight_multiplier).
  >
  >ASSUMPTIONS
  >
  >DECISIONS
  >
  >REFERENCES
  >
  >COMMENTS
  >
  >
  >*/

/* NOTE(built_in_bmi_alteration_specs, EN)
	>
	>PARAMETER	BMI model back-end alterations
	>LOCATION	Base(BMIcore).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	March 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	This parameter is used to make adjustment to the BMI model.  The
	adjustment are made after the BMI model has done its thing, so the BMI equation
	is not altered, per se, but its results are.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[SEX][from_25_to_80_by_5_t][bmi_built_in_alteration_specs_t]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	The parameter was used so that the BMI model gives results that are
	closer to CCHS for years up to 2016.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS
	>
	>
	>
	>*/

/* NOTE(table_filter_age, EN)
	>
	>PARAMETER
	>Tabulation restriction: Age (current)
	>
	>LOCATION	Base(Tabulation_Control).dat
	>LAST UPDATE	June 2019
	>
	>DESCRIPTION	Range of ages for which tabulation should be performed.
	>
	>DIMENSIONS
	>[c_lower_upper_bounds_t]
	>
	>
	>SOURCE
	>
	>DEFINITION
	>
	>METHODS	Tabulation in this model is restricted to a population of interest define
	by the following set of 9 parameters.
	>*Sex(es) must be within those selected in parameter "Tabulation restriction:
	Sex".
	>*Province(s) must be within those selected in parameter "Tabulation
	restriction: Province".
	>*Education level(s) must be within those selected in parameter "Tabulation
	restriction: Education".
	>*Income level(s) must be within those selected in parameter "Tabulation
	restriction: Income quartile".
	>*Point(s) of entry must be within those selected in parameter "Tabulation
	restriction: Entry point/door".
	>*Age must be within the bounds provided by parameter "Tabulation restriction:
	Age (current)".
	>*Initial age must be within the bounds provided by parameter "Tabulation
	restriction: Initial age (upon actor creation)".
	>*Year must be within the bounds provided by parameter "Tabulation
	restriction: Year (current)".
	>*Initial year must be within the bounds provided by parameter "Tabulation
	restriction: Initial year (upon actor creation)".
	>
	>Note that some tables may have additional conditions that further restrict the
	population tabulated.
	>
	>ASSUMPTIONS
	>
	>DECISIONS
	>
	>REFERENCES
	>
	>COMMENTS	The default scenario calls for no specific restriction.  Therefore
	only "additional conditions" specific to each table will restrict the
	population tabulated.
	>
	>*/
